WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.130
Film Courage:&nbsp; Markus I was going through
the AFM Twitter and I just found this article

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.580
entitled “Thirteen Frequently Used Phrases
That Make You Sound Like a Hollywood Rookie”

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.000
by Stephanie Palmer.&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.730
And it’s a funny article, there are some
really interesting things in here that I actually

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:19.270
would not have known that if said, would make
someone sound like a rookie, so….13 Phrases

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.720
to Avoid (she writes): 1.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.050
High Concept…and I’m just wondering what
your thoughts are because I know you’ll

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.820
have something interesting to say.&nbsp;

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.340
She says that if your idea is High Concept
it’s obvious.&nbsp;

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:32.790
Saying it won’t help.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:33.790
2.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:43.329
We’ve had a lot of interest…Which means
lots of people have read it, but none of them

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:47.000
have liked it to get involved.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.000
3.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.760
With the right cast….Everyone knows this,
basically.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:51.760
4.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.469
This is a very unique project…which means
it’s a red flag.&nbsp;

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:00.280
If it’s unique you haven’t recent to understand
the genre.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:01.280
5.&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.880
There is another point that Film Courage likes
from this article “It’s with a message.”&nbsp;

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:12.049
Meaning when you’re highlighting that it’s
with a message, this means you’re focusing

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:20.439
primarily on teaching the audience a lesson
instead of telling a great story.

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:21.439
One last one….6.

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:25.349
I’ve been working on this film for 11 years.&nbsp;

00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:29.979
She says you’re committed, o.k., possibly
inept.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:36.779
We’re having fun with this article, but
how much truth do you hear on what the writer

00:01:36.779 --> 00:01:37.779
is saying?

00:01:37.779 --> 00:01:39.779
Markus Redmond:&nbsp; I think…(pauses).&nbsp;

00:01:39.779 --> 00:01:43.939
God bless everybody.&nbsp;

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:53.799
These articles, I take them all with a grain
of salt because there is no road.&nbsp;

00:01:53.799 --> 00:02:01.789
I mean to say that these things are going
to make you look like…is to indicate that

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:05.819
these are the things you do correctly and
if you do these things correctly, things will

00:02:05.819 --> 00:02:06.959
go the right way.&nbsp;

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.300
And there is no “correct.”&nbsp;

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.680
The only “correct” is someone writing
you a check.&nbsp;

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:21.409
You can say this is a really unique story
and someone can say ‘I agree with you’

00:02:21.409 --> 00:02:22.760
and then they write you a check.&nbsp;

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.840
Which makes that completely null and void.&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.709
You can say, ‘We’ve had a lot of interest’
and someone can say “Well, let me look at

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:32.909
it, I’m interested.”&nbsp;

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:37.709
And then they write a check, which makes that
saying null and void.&nbsp;

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:44.180
You can’t say the right thing to the wrong
person.&nbsp;

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:52.939
I think that is the real, only thing.&nbsp;

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:59.069
You can’t say the right thing to the wrong
person and you can’t say the wrong thing

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:02.239
to the right person.&nbsp;

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:07.159
I think that means that if it’s not going
to work for somebody, you can’t make it

00:03:07.159 --> 00:03:10.480
work for somebody, you know?&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:17.830
I don’t think how you say something if it’s
coming from a place of truth in you, is going

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.249
to make the difference.&nbsp;

00:03:19.249 --> 00:03:24.830
Like if you’ve got an incredible script
and you’ve got like a really good set of

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:30.579
whatever you’re supposed to have behind
it but you use one of these phrases, someone

00:03:30.579 --> 00:03:35.450
who really is in love with your project isn’t
going to say ‘God, I love this script and

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.980
he’s got so much great energy and I really
want to work with this person but he used

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:39.980
a rookie term….hhmmm.&nbsp;

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:40.980
Nah…

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.829
I better not because he used a rookie term.”&nbsp;

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:47.660
Nobody is going to do that, you know what
I mean?&nbsp;

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.189
You either find the people who are your tribe.

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:57.409
You find the people who dig what it is that
you’re looking to do or you don’t.&nbsp;

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:03.799
And in that point, you either decide, I’m
just going to keep looking or you throw in

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:04.799
the towel.&nbsp;

00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:11.799
But I don’t think it comes down to say this
or that, or I don’t know.&nbsp;

00:04:11.799 --> 00:04:13.139
Maybe it does.&nbsp;

00:04:13.139 --> 00:04:18.139
I just think that if you’re coming from
a real place and you’re putting your best

00:04:18.139 --> 00:04:23.680
work out there, you just got to keep pushing
until you find the right person.&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:24.680
You know…?

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:25.680
Question For the Viewers:&nbsp; Is there a rookie
mistake that cost you advancement in your

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:25.681
career?

